{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_divisor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\" For a given number n, find the largest number that divides n evenly, smaller than n.\n    >>> largest_divisor(15)\n    5\n    \"\"\"\n    import math\n    max_div = 1\n    root = int(math.isqrt(n))\n    for i in range(2, root + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            max_div = max(max_div, i, n // i)\n    return max_div\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "def largest_divisor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Finds the largest proper divisor for an integer n.\n    It searches for divisors up to the integer square root of n.\"\"\"\n    best_divisor = 1\n    # Iterate from 2 up to the integer square root of n.\n    # If no divisor is found in this range, n is prime, and 1 is correctly returned.\n    for i in range(2, int(n**0.5) + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            # Both i and n // i are proper divisors for n > 1.\n            # Update best_divisor with the largest of the two found.\n            best_divisor = max(best_divisor, i, n // i)\n    return best_divisor"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Return list of prime factors of given integer in the order from smallest to largest.\n    Each of the factors should be listed number of times corresponding to how many times it appeares in factorization.\n    Input number should be equal to the product of all factors\n    >>> factorize(8)\n    [2, 2, 2]\n    >>> factorize(25)\n    [5, 5]\n    >>> factorize(70)\n    [2, 5, 7]\n    \"\"\"\n    factors: List[int] = []\n\n    # Handle the case of n=1 specifically as per design.\n    if n == 1:\n        return []\n\n    # Factor out 2s\n    while n % 2 == 0:\n        factors.append(2)\n        n //= 2\n\n    # Factor out odd numbers\n    f = 3\n    # Check for factors up to the square root of n.\n    while f * f <= n:\n        while n % f == 0:\n            factors.append(f)\n            n //= f\n        f += 2\n\n    # If n is still greater than 1, it must be a prime factor itself.\n    if n > 1:\n        factors.append(n)\n\n    return factors\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/26", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef remove_duplicates(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" From a list of integers, remove all elements that occur more than once.\n    Keep order of elements left the same as in the input.\n    >>> remove_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 2, 4])\n    [1, 3, 4]\n    \"\"\"\n    counts = {}\n    for num in numbers:\n        counts[num] = counts.get(num, 0) + 1\n\n    result = []\n    for num in numbers:\n        if counts[num] == 1:\n            result.append(num)\n\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/26", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef remove_duplicates(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" From a list of integers, remove all elements that occur more than once.\n    Keep order of elements left the same as in the input.\n    >>> remove_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 2, 4])\n    [1, 3, 4]\n    \"\"\"\n    counts = {}\n    for v in numbers:\n        counts[v] = counts.get(v, 0) + 1\n    return [v for v in numbers if counts.get(v, 0) == 1]\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "```python\n# pyproject.toml\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=42\", \"wheel\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n[project]\nname = \"primefactor\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"Prime factorization library and CLI\"\nreadme = \"README.md\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.9\"\nlicense = { text = \"MIT\" }\nkeywords = [\"prime\", \"factorization\", \"math\", \"cli\"]\nauthors = [\n  { name = \"Your Name\", email = \"your.email@example.com\" },\n]\nclassifiers = [\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3\",\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3.9\",\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3.10\",\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3.11\",\n    \"License :: OSI Approved :: MIT License\",\n    \"Operating System :: OS Independent\",\n    \"Intended Audience :: Developers\",\n    \"Topic :: Scientific/Engineering :: Mathematics\",\n    \"Topic :: Software Development :: Libraries :: Python Modules\",\n    \"Topic :: Utilities\",\n]\ndependencies = []\n\n[project.urls]\nHomepage = \"https://github.com/your-username/primefactor\"\nRepository = \"https://github.com/your-username/primefactor\"\n\n[project.scripts]\nprimefactor = \"primefactor.__init__:cli_entrypoint\"\n\n[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\nwhere = [\"src\"]\n\n[tool.pytest.ini_options]\nminversion = \"6.0\"\naddopts = \"-q\"\ntestpaths = [\n    \"tests\",\n]\n```\n```python\n# README.md\n# Primefactor\n\nA Python library and CLI tool for computing prime factorizations of positive integers.\n\n## Features\n\n- Reliable prime factorization for integers >= 2.\n- Library API (`primefactor.factorize(n)`).\n- CLI tool (`primefactor factorize <n>`).\n- Caching of recent results for performance.\n- Automated tests and CI.\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\npip install primefactor\n```\n\n## Usage\n\n### As a Library\n\n```python\nfrom primefactor import factorize\n\n# Compute prime factors\nfactors = factorize(70)\nprint(factors) # Output: [2, 5, 7]\n\nfactors_cached = factorize(70) # Potentially faster if cache enabled\nprint(factors_cached) # Output: [2, 5, 7]\n\ntry:\n    factorize(1)\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(e) # Output: n must be integer >= 2, got: 1\n```\n\n### As a CLI Tool\n\n```bash\n# Factorize a number\nprimefactor factorize 70\n# Output: [2, 5, 7]\n\n# Factorize a number with caching enabled (default path: ~/.cache/primefactor/cache.json)\nprimefactor factorize 8 --cache\n# Output: [2, 2, 2]\n\n# Factorize a number with a custom cache path\nprimefactor factorize 25 --cache /tmp/my_prime_cache.json\n# Output: [5, 5]\n\n# Disable caching for a specific call\nprimefactor factorize 100 --no-cache\n# Output: [2, 2, 5, 5]\n\n# Invalid input handling\nprimefactor factorize 1\n# Output (to stderr): Error: n must be integer >= 2, got: 1\n# Exit code: 2\n```\n\n## Development\n\nTo set up a development environment:\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/your-username/primefactor.git\ncd primefactor\npip install -e \".[dev]\" # Or just pip install -e . and pip install pytest\npytest\n```\n```\n```yaml\n# .github/workflows/ci.yml\nname: CI\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [ \"main\" ]\n  pull_request:\n    branches: [ \"main\" ]\n\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    strategy:\n      matrix:\n        python-version: [\"3.9\", \"3.10\", \"3.11\"]\n\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - name: Set up Python ${{ matrix.python-version }}\n      uses: actions/setup-python@v4\n      with:\n        python-version: ${{ matrix.python-version }}\n    - name: Install dependencies\n      run: |\n        python -m pip install --upgrade pip\n        pip install \"pytest>=6.0\" setuptools wheel\n        pip install -e .  # Install the package itself in editable mode\n    - name: Run tests\n      run: |\n        pytest\n```\n```python\n# src/primefactor/__init__.py\nfrom typing import List, Optional\n\nfrom .controllers import FactorizeController\nfrom .repositories import NullRepository\nfrom .cli import CLI\n\n# Package version\n__version__ = \"0.1.0\"\n\ndef factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the prime factorization of a positive integer n.\n    Factors are sorted ascending and repeated by multiplicity.\n    \"\"\"\n    # By default, the public API does not use a file-backed cache.\n    # A NullRepository is injected to PrimeFactorizer via the controller.\n    controller = FactorizeController(repository=NullRepository())\n    return controller.factorize(n)\n\ndef cli_entrypoint(argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Entrypoint for the command-line interface.\n    It creates a CLI instance and runs it with the provided arguments.\n    \"\"\"\n    return CLI().run(argv)\n```\n```python\n# src/primefactor/cli.py\nimport argparse\nimport json\nimport logging\nimport os\nimport sys\nfrom typing import List, Optional\n\nfrom .controllers import FactorizeController\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidInputError\nfrom .repositories import PrimeRepository # For default cache path\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\nclass CLI:\n    \"\"\"Command-line interface for the primefactor tool.\"\"\"\n    DEFAULT_CACHE_ENV: str = \"PRIMEFACTOR_CACHE\"\n\n    def run(self, argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Parses arguments, executes the factorization, and prints JSON output.\"\"\"\n        parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n            prog=\"primefactor\",\n            description=\"A CLI tool for prime factorization.\"\n        )\n        subparsers = parser.add_subparsers(dest=\"command\", help=\"Available commands\")\n\n        # Factorize command\n        factorize_parser = subparsers.add_parser(\n            \"factorize\",\n            help=\"Factorize a positive integer.\"\n        )\n        factorize_parser.add_argument(\n            \"n\",\n            type=int,\n            help=\"The positive integer to factorize (n >= 2).\"\n        )\n        cache_group = factorize_parser.add_mutually_exclusive_group()\n        cache_group.add_argument(\n            \"--cache\",\n            type=str,\n            nargs=\"?\", # 0 or 1 argument\n            const=PrimeRepository.DEFAULT_CACHE_PATH, # If flag is present without value, use default\n            default=None, # Default if flag not present at all\n            help=f\"Specify cache file path. If no path given, uses default ({PrimeRepository.DEFAULT_CACHE_PATH}). \"\n                 f\"Overrides {self.DEFAULT_CACHE_ENV} environment variable.\"\n        )\n        cache_group.add_argument(\n            \"--no-cache\",\n            action=\"store_true\",\n            help=\"Disable caching for this operation.\"\n        )\n\n        args = parser.parse_args(argv)\n\n        if args.command == \"factorize\":\n            controller = FactorizeController()\n            try:\n                # Determine cache path for controller\n                resolved_cache_path = args.cache\n                if resolved_cache_path is None and not args.no_cache:\n                    # If --cache was not specified, and --no-cache is not true, check env var\n                    env_cache_path = os.environ.get(self.DEFAULT_CACHE_ENV)\n                    if env_cache_path:\n                        resolved_cache_path = env_cache_path\n\n                result = controller.factorize_with_cache_option(\n                    args.n,\n                    resolved_cache_path,\n                    disable_cache=args.no_cache\n                )\n                self._print_json(result)\n                return 0\n            except InvalidInputError as e:\n                print(f\"Error: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n                return 2 # Specific exit code for invalid input\n            except Exception as e:\n                logger.error(f\"An unexpected error occurred: {e}\", exc_info=True)\n                print(f\"An unexpected error occurred: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n                return 1 # General error\n        else:\n            parser.print_help(sys.stderr)\n            return 1 # No command given or unknown command\n\n    def _print_json(self, result: List[int]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Prints the factorization result as a JSON array to stdout.\"\"\"\n        print(json.dumps(result))\n\n# Configure logging for the CLI, to make repository messages visible\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.WARNING, format='%(levelname)s: %(message)s')\n```\n```python\n# src/primefactor/controllers.py\nfrom typing import List\nimport os\nfrom .services import PrimeFactorizer\nfrom .repositories import PrimeRepository, NullRepository\nfrom .utils import Utils\n\nclass FactorizeController:\n    \"\"\"Orchestrates input validation, service invocation, and repository usage.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, repository: 'PrimeRepository | NullRepository | None' = None):\n        \"\"\"Initializes the controller with an optional repository dependency.\"\"\"\n        self.repository = repository\n\n    def factorize(self, n: int) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Computes prime factors for n, typically used by the library API (no explicit cache path).\"\"\"\n        Utils.validate_input(n)\n        # Use NullRepository if no specific repository is injected\n        repo = self.repository or NullRepository()\n        service = PrimeFactorizer(repository=repo)\n        return service.compute_factors(n)\n\n    def factorize_with_cache_option(self, n: int, cache_path: str | None, disable_cache: bool = False) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Computes prime factors, allowing configuration of cache path or disabling cache.\"\"\"\n        Utils.validate_input(n)\n        repo: 'PrimeRepository | NullRepository'\n\n        if disable_cache:\n            repo = NullRepository()\n        elif cache_path:\n            repo = PrimeRepository(cache_path)\n        else: # Default behavior - check env var or use default path\n            env_cache_path = os.environ.get(\"PRIMEFACTOR_CACHE\")\n            if env_cache_path:\n                repo = PrimeRepository(env_cache_path)\n            else:\n                repo = PrimeRepository(PrimeRepository.DEFAULT_CACHE_PATH)\n        \n        service = PrimeFactorizer(repository=repo)\n        return service.compute_factors(n)\n```\n```python\n# src/primefactor/services.py\nfrom typing import List\nfrom .utils import Utils\nfrom .repositories import NullRepository, PrimeRepository # Import for type hinting, actual objects passed\n\nclass PrimeFactorizer:\n    \"\"\"Core service for computing prime factorizations, utilizing a repository for caching.\"\"\"\n    MAX_DIRECT_TRIAL: int = 2**63 - 1 # Documented limit for 64-bit integers\n\n    def __init__(self, repository: 'PrimeRepository | NullRepository | None' = None):\n        \"\"\"Initializes the PrimeFactorizer with an optional repository.\"\"\"\n        self.repository = repository or NullRepository()\n\n    def compute_factors(self, n: int) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Returns the ordered list of prime factors for n, using cache if available.\"\"\"\n        Utils.validate_input(n) \n\n        cached = self.repository.get(n)\n        if cached is not None:\n            return cached\n        \n        factors = self._trial_division(n)\n        self.repository.set(n, factors)\n        return factors\n\n    def _trial_division(self, n: int) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Performs trial division to find prime factors, optimized for common cases.\"\"\"\n        factors: List[int] = []\n\n        # Divide out factor 2\n        while n % 2 == 0:\n            factors.append(2)\n            n //= 2\n        \n        # Iterate odd divisors from 3 up to sqrt(n)\n        d = 3\n        limit = Utils.integer_sqrt(n)\n        while d <= limit and n > 1:\n            while n % d == 0:\n                factors.append(d)\n                n //= d\n                # Update limit because n has become smaller\n                limit = Utils.integer_sqrt(n)\n            d += 2\n        \n        # If n is still greater than 1, it must be a prime factor itself\n        if n > 1:\n            factors.append(n)\n            \n        return factors\n```\n```python\n# src/primefactor/repositories.py\nimport json\nimport logging\nimport os\nimport shutil\nimport time\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Any\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\nclass PrimeRepository:\n    \"\"\"Manages persistence and retrieval of prime factorizations using a JSON file.\"\"\"\n    DEFAULT_CACHE_PATH: str = \"~/.cache/primefactor/cache.json\"\n    CACHE_FILE_VERSION: int = 1\n    MAX_BACKUPS: int = 3 # Keep last N backups\n\n    def __init__(self, cache_path: str):\n        self.cache_path = Path(cache_path).expanduser()\n        self.cache: Dict[str, List[int]] = {}\n        self._load_cache()\n\n    def get(self, n: int) -> List[int] | None:\n        \"\"\"Retrieves factors for n from the cache, if available.\"\"\"\n        return self.cache.get(str(n))\n\n    def set(self, n: int, factors: List[int]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Stores factors for n in the cache and persists it to disk.\"\"\"\n        self.cache[str(n)] = factors\n        self._persist_cache()\n\n    def _load_cache(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Loads the cache from the JSON file. Handles missing or corrupt files.\"\"\"\n        if not self.cache_path.exists():\n            logger.info(f\"Cache file not found at {self.cache_path}. Initializing empty cache.\")\n            self.cache = {}\n            return\n\n        try:\n            with open(self.cache_path, 'r', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n                data: Dict[str, Any] = json.load(f)\n            \n            # Check version\n            if data.get(\"version\") != self.CACHE_FILE_VERSION:\n                logger.warning(\n                    f\"Cache file version mismatch. Expected {self.CACHE_FILE_VERSION}, \"\n                    f\"got {data.get('version')}. Rebuilding cache.\"\n                )\n                self._backup_and_reset_cache(f\"version_mismatch_{data.get('version')}\")\n                return\n            \n            self.cache = data.get(\"entries\", {})\n            logger.info(f\"Cache loaded from {self.cache_path} with {len(self.cache)} entries.\")\n\n        except (json.JSONDecodeError, FileNotFoundError, IOError) as e:\n            logger.warning(f\"Failed to load cache from {self.cache_path}: {e}. Rebuilding cache.\")\n            self._backup_and_reset_cache(\"load_error\")\n        except Exception as e:\n            logger.error(f\"An unexpected error occurred while loading cache from {self.cache_path}: {e}. Rebuilding cache.\", exc_info=True)\n            self._backup_and_reset_cache(\"unexpected_error\")\n\n    def _persist_cache(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Persists the in-memory cache to the JSON file using atomic write.\"\"\"\n        self.cache_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        \n        temp_path = self.cache_path.with_suffix('.json.tmp')\n        data_to_save = {\n            \"version\": self.CACHE_FILE_VERSION,\n            \"generated_at\": time.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\", time.gmtime()),\n            \"entries\": self.cache\n        }\n        try:\n            with open(temp_path, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n                json.dump(data_to_save, f, indent=2)\n            \n            # Atomic replacement\n            if self.cache_path.exists():\n                self._create_backup()\n            os.replace(temp_path, self.cache_path)\n            os.chmod(self.cache_path, 0o600) # Set restrictive permissions\n            logger.debug(f\"Cache successfully persisted to {self.cache_path}.\")\n        except IOError as e:\n            logger.error(f\"Failed to persist cache to {self.cache_path}: {e}\")\n            if temp_path.exists():\n                os.remove(temp_path) # Clean up temp file on error\n        except Exception as e:\n            logger.error(f\"An unexpected error occurred during cache persistence to {self.cache_path}: {e}\", exc_info=True)\n            if temp_path.exists():\n                os.remove(temp_path)\n\n    def _create_backup(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Creates a timestamped backup of the current cache file.\"\"\"\n        backup_dir = self.cache_path.parent / \"backups\"\n        backup_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        timestamp = time.strftime(\"%Y%m%d%H%M%S\", time.gmtime())\n        backup_path = backup_dir / f\"{self.cache_path.name}.bak.{timestamp}\"\n        try:\n            shutil.copy2(self.cache_path, backup_path)\n            logger.debug(f\"Created cache backup: {backup_path}\")\n            self._prune_backups(backup_dir)\n        except Exception as e:\n            logger.warning(f\"Could not create cache backup for {self.cache_path}: {e}\")\n\n    def _prune_backups(self, backup_dir: Path) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Removes old backup files, keeping only the most recent.\"\"\"\n        backups = sorted(\n            [f for f in backup_dir.iterdir() if f.name.startswith(f\"{self.cache_path.name}.bak.\")],\n            key=lambda p: p.stat().st_mtime,\n            reverse=True\n        )\n        for old_backup in backups[self.MAX_BACKUPS:]:\n            try:\n                os.remove(old_backup)\n                logger.debug(f\"Removed old cache backup: {old_backup}\")\n            except Exception as e:\n                logger.warning(f\"Could not remove old cache backup {old_backup}: {e}\")\n\n    def _backup_and_reset_cache(self, reason: str) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Backs up the current cache file and resets the in-memory cache.\"\"\"\n        if self.cache_path.exists():\n            backup_dir = self.cache_path.parent / \"backups\"\n            backup_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n            timestamp = time.strftime(\"%Y%m%d%H%M%S\", time.gmtime())\n            corrupt_path = backup_dir / f\"{self.cache_path.name}.corrupt_{reason}.{timestamp}\"\n            try:\n                shutil.move(self.cache_path, corrupt_path)\n                logger.warning(f\"Moved corrupt cache file to {corrupt_path}\")\n            except Exception as e:\n                logger.error(f\"Could not move corrupt cache file {self.cache_path}: {e}\", exc_info=True)\n        self.cache = {}\n\n\nclass NullRepository:\n    \"\"\"A no-op repository used when caching is disabled.\"\"\"\n    def get(self, n: int) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Returns None, indicating no cached factors are found.\"\"\"\n        return None\n\n    def set(self, n: int, factors: List[int]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Does nothing, as caching is disabled.\"\"\"\n        pass\n```\n```python\n# src/primefactor/models.py\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import List\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass FactorizationResult:\n    \"\"\"Data Transfer Object for factorization results.\"\"\"\n    n: int\n    factors: List[int]\n\n    def product(self) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the product of the factors for verification.\"\"\"\n        prod = 1\n        for f in self.factors:\n            prod *= f\n        return prod\n```\n```python\n# src/primefactor/utils.py\nimport math\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\nclass Utils:\n    \"\"\"Provides utility methods for input validation and numeric operations.\"\"\"\n    MIN_INPUT: int = 2\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def validate_input(n: int) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Validates if n is an integer and meets the minimum input requirement.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(n, int) or n < Utils.MIN_INPUT:\n            raise InvalidInputError(f\"n must be integer >= {Utils.MIN_INPUT}, got: {n}\")\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def integer_sqrt(n: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Computes the integer square root of n (floor(sqrt(n))).\"\"\"\n        return math.isqrt(n)\n    \n    @staticmethod\n    def is_prime_small(n: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if a small integer n is prime. Not optimized for large numbers.\"\"\"\n        if n < 2: return False\n        if n == 2 or n == 3: return True\n        if n % 2 == 0 or n % 3 == 0: return False\n        i = 5\n        while i * i <= n:\n            if n % i == 0 or n % (i + 2) == 0:\n                return False\n            i += 6\n        return True\n```\n```python\n# src/primefactor/exceptions.py\nclass InvalidInputError(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Exception raised for invalid input to factorization functions.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str):\n        super().__init__(message)\n        self.message = message\n\n    def __str__(self) -> str:\n        return self.message\n```\n```python\n# tests/test_factorizer.py\nimport pytest\nimport math\nfrom primefactor import factorize\nfrom primefactor.exceptions import InvalidInputError\nfrom primefactor.services import PrimeFactorizer\nfrom primefactor.utils import Utils\nfrom primefactor.repositories import NullRepository, PrimeRepository\nimport os\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\n\n# --- Tests for public API factorize ---\ndef test_factorize_examples():\n    \"\"\"Verify common examples of factorization.\"\"\"\n    assert factorize(2) == [2]\n    assert factorize(8) == [2, 2, 2]\n    assert factorize(25) == [5, 5]\n    assert factorize(70) == [2, 5, 7]\n    assert factorize(100) == [2, 2, 5, 5]\n    assert factorize(997) == [997] # A prime number\n    assert factorize(999) == [3, 3, 3, 37]\n    assert factorize(2**63 - 1) == [7, 73, 127, 337, 92737, 649657] # Mersenne prime M63\n\ndef test_factorize_product_invariant():\n    \"\"\"Ensure the product of factors equals the original input.\"\"\"\n    test_numbers = [2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 60, 997, 1024, 123456789]\n    for n in test_numbers:\n        factors = factorize(n)\n        prod = 1\n        for f in factors:\n            prod *= f\n        assert prod == n\n\ndef test_factorize_invalid_input():\n    \"\"\"Test that InvalidInputError is raised for invalid inputs.\"\"\"\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=\"must be integer >= 2\"):\n        factorize(1)\n    with py