{"task_id": "HumanEval/75", "solution": "def is_multiply_prime(a):\n    \"\"\"Checks if a number is a product of exactly three prime numbers.\n    Assumes a is an integer between 1 and 99, inclusive.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _sieve_primes(limit):\n        \"\"\"Generates a list of primes up to the given limit using the Sieve of Eratosthenes.\"\"\"\n        sieve = [True] * (limit + 1)\n        if limit >= 0: sieve[0] = False\n        if limit >= 1: sieve[1] = False\n        for p in range(2, int(limit**0.5) + 1):\n            if sieve[p]:\n                for multiple in range(p*p, limit + 1, p):\n                    sieve[multiple] = False\n        return [i for i, is_p in enumerate(sieve) if is_p]\n\n    def _find_prime_factors(n, primes):\n        \"\"\"Finds the prime factors of n, including multiplicities, using a list of primes.\"\"\"\n        factors = []\n        temp_n = n\n        for p in primes:\n            while temp_n % p == 0:\n                factors.append(p)\n                temp_n //= p\n            if temp_n == 1:\n                break\n        if temp_n > 1: # If remaining temp_n is greater than 1, it must be a prime itself.\n            factors.append(temp_n)\n        return factors\n\n    # The input 'a' is less than 100, so the maximum value for 'a' is 99.\n    # We generate primes up to 99 to ensure all possible factors can be found.\n    all_primes = _sieve_primes(99)\n\n    factors = _find_prime_factors(a, all_primes)\n\n    return len(factors) == 3"}
