{"task_id": "HumanEval/101", "solution": "```python\nimport re\n\ndef words_string(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    You will be given a string of words separated by commas or spaces. Your task is\n    to split the string into words and return an array of the words.\n    \n    For example:\n    words_string(\"Hi, my name is John\") == [\"Hi\", \"my\", \"name\", \"is\", \"John\"]\n    words_string(\"One, two, three, four, five, six\") == [\"One\", \"two\", \"three\", \"four\", \"five\", \"six\"]\n    \"\"\"\n    # Split the string using a regex that matches one or more commas or whitespace characters.\n    # Then, strip leading/trailing whitespace from each token and filter out any empty strings.\n    tokens = re.split(r'[,\\s]+', s)\n    return [token.strip() for token in tokens if token.strip()]\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/102", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef choose_num(x, y):\n    \"\"\"This function finds the largest even integer in the range [x, y] inclusive.\n    If no such number exists, it returns -1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if x > y:\n        return -1\n\n    # Find the largest possible integer within the range [x, y]\n    candidate = math.floor(y)\n\n    # Check the candidate and potentially the one before it\n    if candidate < math.ceil(x): # No integers in range\n        return -1\n    \n    if (candidate % 2 == 0):\n        if candidate >= math.ceil(x):\n            return int(candidate)\n    \n    # If candidate was odd or too small, try the previous integer\n    candidate -= 1\n    if (candidate % 2 == 0):\n        if candidate >= math.ceil(x):\n            return int(candidate)\n            \n    return -1\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/102", "solution": "import math\n\ndef choose_num(x, y):\n    \"\"\"This function returns the biggest even integer in [x, y] inclusive.\n    Returns -1 if no such number or if the interval is invalid (x > y).\n    \"\"\"\n    if x > y:\n        return -1\n\n    largest_possible_int = math.floor(y)\n\n    candidate_even = largest_possible_int\n    if candidate_even % 2 != 0:\n        candidate_even -= 1\n\n    if candidate_even >= x:\n        return int(candidate_even)\n    else:\n        return -1"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/103", "solution": "```python\ndef rounded_avg(n, m):\n    \"\"\"Computes the rounded average of integers from n through m, returning it in binary string format.\n    Returns -1 if n is greater than m.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n > m:\n        return -1\n\n    # Calculate the average of an arithmetic series (n through m inclusive)\n    # The average is simply the sum of the first and last term, divided by two.\n    average_float = (n + m) / 2.0\n\n    # Round the average to the nearest integer, using 'round halves up' rule (e.g., 3.5 rounds to 4).\n    # For non-negative values, adding 0.5 and then truncating to an integer achieves this.\n    rounded_average = int(average_float + 0.5)\n\n    # Convert the rounded integer to its binary string representation, prefixed with \"0b\".\n    return bin(rounded_average)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/103", "solution": "def rounded_avg(n, m):\n    \"\"\"Computes the rounded average of integers from n through m, converts to binary.\n    Returns -1 if n > m.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n > m:\n        return -1\n\n    # Calculate count of numbers in the range [n, m]\n    count = m - n + 1\n\n    # Calculate sum of numbers in the range [n, m] using arithmetic series formula\n    # (count * (first + last)) // 2\n    total_sum = (count * (n + m)) // 2\n\n    # Compute the average (float division)\n    average = total_sum / count\n\n    # Round to the nearest integer. Python's round() implements \"round half to even\".\n    # This matches the provided examples (e.g., 26.5 rounds to 26).\n    rounded_value = round(average)\n\n    # Convert the rounded integer to a Python binary string (e.g., \"0b11\")\n    return bin(rounded_value)"}
