{"task_id": "HumanEval/136", "solution": "def largest_smallest_integers(lst):\n    '''\n    Finds the largest negative and smallest positive integers in a list.\n    Returns (largest_negative, smallest_positive), or None if none exist.\n    '''\n    largest_negative = None\n    smallest_positive = None\n\n    for n in lst:\n        if n < 0:\n            if largest_negative is None or n > largest_negative:\n                largest_negative = n\n        elif n > 0:\n            if smallest_positive is None or n < smallest_positive:\n                smallest_positive = n\n\n    return (largest_negative, smallest_positive)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/136", "solution": "def largest_smallest_integers(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the largest negative integer and smallest positive integer from a list.\n    Ignores non-integer values and zero; returns None if no such integers are found.\n    \"\"\"\n    largest_negative = None\n    smallest_positive = None\n\n    for value in lst:\n        # Convert the value to float to check for integer property.\n        # This trusts that the input 'value' is convertible to a float.\n        f_value = float(value)\n\n        # Skip if the float value is not an integer-valued number (e.g., 3.5).\n        if not f_value.is_integer():\n            continue\n\n        # Convert to int after confirming it's an integer value.\n        i_value = int(f_value)\n\n        # Ignore zero as per the business rules.\n        if i_value == 0:\n            continue\n\n        if i_value < 0:\n            if largest_negative is None or i_value > largest_negative:\n                largest_negative = i_value\n        else:  # i_value > 0\n            if smallest_positive is None or i_value < smallest_positive:\n                smallest_positive = i_value\n\n    return (largest_negative, smallest_positive)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/137", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef compare_one(a, b):\n    \"\"\"\n    Compares two values (int, float, or numeric string) and returns the larger in its original type.\n    Returns None if the values are numerically equal, supporting '.' and ',' as decimal separators.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    # Define a small epsilon for floating-point comparisons to account for precision issues.\n    EPSILON = 1e-9\n\n    # Helper function to convert a string representing a real number into a float.\n    # It normalizes decimal separators from ',' to '.' before conversion.\n    def _parse_numeric_string(value_str: str) -> float:\n        return float(value_str.strip().replace(',', '.'))\n\n    # Convert inputs to their numeric (float) representation for comparison,\n    # while retaining the original input values for potential return.\n    original_a = a\n    original_b = b\n\n    numeric_a: float\n    numeric_b: float\n\n    if isinstance(a, str):\n        numeric_a = _parse_numeric_string(a)\n    elif isinstance(a, int):\n        numeric_a = float(a)\n    else: # Based on problem statement, 'a' must be a float if not int or str.\n        numeric_a = a\n\n    if isinstance(b, str):\n        numeric_b = _parse_numeric_string(b)\n    elif isinstance(b, int):\n        numeric_b = float(b)\n    else: # Based on problem statement, 'b' must be a float if not int or str.\n        numeric_b = b\n\n    # Compare the numeric values with a tolerance for floating-point equality.\n    if math.fabs(numeric_a - numeric_b) <= EPSILON:\n        return None # Values are numerically equal.\n\n    # Determine which value is larger and prepare to return it in its original type.\n    if numeric_a > numeric_b:\n        if isinstance(original_a, str):\n            return original_a # If original was a string, return the exact string.\n        # If original was an int and its numeric value is essentially an integer, return as int.\n        if isinstance(original_a, int) and math.fabs(numeric_a - round(numeric_a)) <= EPSILON:\n            return int(round(numeric_a))\n        return float(numeric_a) # Otherwise, return as float.\n    else:\n        if isinstance(original_b, str):\n            return original_b\n        if isinstance(original_b, int) and math.fabs(numeric_b - round(numeric_b)) <= EPSILON:\n            return int(round(numeric_b))\n        return float(numeric_b)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/137", "solution": "```python\ndef compare_one(a, b):\n    \"\"\"\n    Compares two values (int, float, or string representations of numbers) and returns the numerically larger one,\n    preserving its original type. Returns None if the values are numerically equal.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # Helper function to convert input to a float for comparison.\n    # Handles comma as a decimal separator for strings according to the problem statement.\n    def _parse_to_float(value):\n        return float(str(value).replace(',', '.'))\n\n    a_num = _parse_to_float(a)\n    b_num = _parse_to_float(b)\n\n    if a_num == b_num:\n        return None\n    elif a_num > b_num:\n        return a\n    else:\n        return b\n```"}
