{"task_id": "HumanEval/147", "solution": "def get_max_triples(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the number of index triples (i < j < k) for n where a[i]+a[j]+a[k] is divisible by 3.\n    a[x] = x*x - x + 1.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _comb(n_val, k_val):\n        \"\"\"Calculates n choose k, returning 0 if k is out of range.\"\"\"\n        if k_val < 0 or n_val < 0 or k_val > n_val:\n            return 0\n        k_val = min(k_val, n_val - k_val)\n        res = 1\n        for i in range(1, k_val + 1):\n            res = res * (n_val - k_val + i) // i\n        return res\n\n    def _count_indices_with_mod3_residue(n_val, residue_target):\n        \"\"\"Counts indices i in 1..n_val such that i % 3 == residue_target.\"\"\"\n        if residue_target == 1:\n            return (n_val + 2) // 3\n        elif residue_target == 2:\n            return (n_val + 1) // 3\n        elif residue_target == 0:\n            return n_val // 3\n        return 0\n\n    # The property of a[i] = i*i - i + 1 modulo 3 is:\n    # a[i] % 3 == 0 if i % 3 == 2\n    # a[i] % 3 == 1 if i % 3 == 0 or i % 3 == 1\n\n    # Count of indices 'idx' in 1..n where a[idx] % 3 == 0.\n    # This corresponds to 'idx' where idx % 3 == 2.\n    count_a_mod_0 = _count_indices_with_mod3_residue(n, 2)\n\n    # Count of indices 'idx' in 1..n where a[idx] % 3 == 1.\n    # This is simply the total 'n' minus 'count_a_mod_0'.\n    count_a_mod_1 = n - count_a_mod_0\n\n    # A sum a[i] + a[j] + a[k] is divisible by 3 if:\n    # 1. All three a values have residue 0 (0+0+0 = 0 mod 3).\n    # 2. All three a values have residue 1 (1+1+1 = 3 mod 3 = 0 mod 3).\n\n    # Calculate combinations for these two cases.\n    triples_000 = _comb(count_a_mod_0, 3)\n    triples_111 = _comb(count_a_mod_1, 3)\n\n    return triples_000 + triples_111"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/148", "solution": "```python\ndef bf(planet1, planet2):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a tuple of planets between two given planets, ordered by proximity to the Sun.\n    Returns an empty tuple if either input planet name is invalid.\n    \"\"\"\n    PLANETS = [\"Mercury\", \"Venus\", \"Earth\", \"Mars\", \"Jupiter\", \"Saturn\", \"Uranus\", \"Neptune\"]\n\n    # Normalize input names to match canonical form (Title-case, stripped whitespace)\n    p1_normalized = planet1.strip().title()\n    p2_normalized = planet2.strip().title()\n\n    # Validate names against the known list of planets\n    if p1_normalized not in PLANETS or p2_normalized not in PLANETS:\n        return ()\n\n    # Find the indices of the normalized planets in the canonical order\n    idx1 = PLANETS.index(p1_normalized)\n    idx2 = PLANETS.index(p2_normalized)\n\n    # Determine the strict start and end indices for the slice, ensuring ascending order\n    start_idx = min(idx1, idx2)\n    end_idx = max(idx1, idx2)\n\n    # If the planets are the same or adjacent, there are no planets strictly between them\n    if end_idx - start_idx <= 1:\n        return ()\n\n    # Extract the planets strictly between the two indices\n    result_planets = PLANETS[start_idx + 1:end_idx]\n\n    return tuple(result_planets)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/148", "solution": "```python\ndef bf(planet1, planet2):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a tuple of planets located between planet1 and planet2, sorted by proximity to the Sun.\n    Returns an empty tuple if either planet name is invalid.\n    \"\"\"\n    PLANETS = [\"Mercury\", \"Venus\", \"Earth\", \"Mars\", \"Jupiter\", \"Saturn\", \"Uranus\", \"Neptune\"]\n    PLANET_ALIASES = {p.lower(): p for p in PLANETS}\n\n    def _normalize_name(name: str) -> str | None:\n        \"\"\"Normalizes a planet name by stripping whitespace and lowercasing, then mapping to canonical name.\"\"\"\n        cleaned_name = name.strip().lower()\n        return PLANET_ALIASES.get(cleaned_name)\n\n    normalized_planet1 = _normalize_name(planet1)\n    normalized_planet2 = _normalize_name(planet2)\n\n    if normalized_planet1 is None or normalized_planet2 is None:\n        return ()\n\n    idx1 = PLANETS.index(normalized_planet1)\n    idx2 = PLANETS.index(normalized_planet2)\n\n    if idx1 == idx2:\n        return ()\n\n    start_idx = min(idx1, idx2)\n    end_idx = max(idx1, idx2)\n\n    result_slice = PLANETS[start_idx + 1:end_idx]\n\n    return tuple(result_slice)\n```"}
